      "content": "You raise a valid point, and it has indeed been the subject of much criticism. To say that Conrad's depiction is \"faithful to the era\" can be misconstrued as excusing offensive language and perpetuating harmful stereotypes. Indeed, Conrad's choice of language reflects the colonial mindset, and using the same language can be seen as an endorsement rather than a critique.\n\nHowever, it is worth noting that Conrad attempted to expose these same stereotypes to reveal their absurdity and cruelty. Marlow's narration attempts to distance itself from the prejudiced expressions of other characters within the book. The character's use of dehumanizing language sometimes serves to heighten the sense of disillusionment and irony rather than to directly critique.\n\nThis duality makes Conrad's work controversial and compelling. He forces readers to confront uncomfortable truths and urgings for colonial power to recognize their own dehumanization of the \"other.\" Critics argue that by reproducing the period's language, Conrad inadvertently affirms racist views, while others cite his intentions to provide a critique and his selective silences in character dialogue as an effort to avoid dehumanizing Africans actively. Indeed, the lines separating imitation from critique in literature are often blurred, and \"Heart of Darkness\" remains a significant",
